latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Identify the shape's boundaries (top, left, bottom, right).\n   b. Calculate the shape's height and width.\n\n2. Create an output grid of the same size as the input grid.\n\n3. For each row of the shape, from top to bottom:\n   a. Calculate the row's relative position (0 at the top, 1 at the bottom).\n   b. Calculate the horizontal shift:\n      - Increase the shift as we move down the rows.\n      - Apply more shift to the right side of the shape than the left.\n   c. Calculate the compression factor:\n      - Maximum compression in the middle rows.\n      - Less compression at the top and bottom rows.\n   d. For each column in the current row:\n      - Calculate the new horizontal position based on the shift and compression.\n      - If the cell is colored (non-zero), transfer it to the new position in the output grid.\n\n4. Post-processing:\n   a. Fill any gaps created within the shape during transformation.\n   b. Ensure the bottom row maintains its original width.\n\n5. Detailed calculations:\n   a. Row position: row_pos = (current_row - top) / (bottom - top)\n   b. Horizontal shift: shift = row_pos * max_shift * (1 + column_relative_pos)\n      where column_relative_pos is 0 for leftmost and 1 for rightmost column\n   c. Compression factor: \n      compression = 1 - max_compression * (1 - abs(2 * row_pos - 1))\n      This creates a parabolic compression with maximum at the middle row\n   d. New column position:\n      new_col = left + (old_col - left) * compression + shift\n\n6. Implementation details:\n   a. Use integer arithmetic for all calculations to avoid floating-point errors.\n   b. Round all calculated positions to the nearest integer.\n   c. Ensure all new positions fall within the grid boundaries.\n\n7. Optimization:\n   a. Pre-calculate and store the shift and compression factors for each row to avoid redundant calculations.\n\n8. Error handling:\n   a. Handle edge cases such as single-row or single-column shapes.\n   b. Ensure the algorithm works correctly for shapes touching the grid edges.\n\nThis plan takes into account the nuanced observations from the reflection, including the non-linear compression and the varying horizontal shift across the shape. It should provide a robust solution that works for all the given examples and generalizes well to other potential cases."
